## 3. Account Roles and Responsibilities

Use the existing role and authentication architecture wherever possible. Map the following roles to the existing system without creating duplicate or conflicting roles.

### A. Super Admin

The Super Admin has complete control over the entire ERP.

Permissions:
- Access every module and all authorized institutional records.
- Create accounts for all supported roles.
- Edit account details.
- Assign and change roles.
- Suspend, reactivate, and deactivate accounts.
- Reset other users' passwords.
- Configure and manage departments and department accounts.
- View all accounts, including the Super Admin account.
- Access all institutional modules and system settings.
- View audit logs and account activity.
- Manage account security and unlock temporarily locked accounts.

The Super Admin must be protected against unauthorized account manipulation by other roles.

### B. Principal

The Principal has access to every institutional module, subject to the explicitly protected Super Admin account.

Permissions:
- Access all institutional modules and their records.
- Create user accounts.
- Edit user account details.
- Assign and change roles, subject to Super Admin protection.
- Suspend and reactivate accounts.
- Deactivate accounts while preserving history.
- Reset user passwords.
- View and manage institutional operations.

Restrictions:
- Must never see the Super Admin account in the User Management interface, search results, account lists, or account selectors.
- Must not suspend, deactivate, modify the role of, or otherwise manipulate the Super Admin account.
- Must not obtain access to Super Admin account details through direct API requests.

### C. Secretary

The Secretary has the same general institutional access and account-management capabilities as the Principal, subject to the same Super Admin protections.

Permissions:
- Access all institutional modules.
- Create and edit user accounts.
- Assign and change roles, subject to Super Admin protection.
- Suspend and reactivate accounts.
- Deactivate accounts while preserving history.
- Reset user passwords.
- View and manage institutional operations.

Restrictions:
- Must never see the Super Admin account in the User Management interface, search results, account lists, or account selectors.
- Must not suspend, deactivate, modify the role of, or otherwise manipulate the Super Admin account.
- Must not obtain access to Super Admin account details through direct API requests.

### D. Office Members

Office members use the standard office role and its defined permissions.

They can:
- Access the office functions required for institutional administration.
- Record fee payments through Fees Management.
- Register new students through the permitted student-record workflow.
- View and edit student details.
- Access the student and financial records required for their duties.

They cannot:
- Access User Management.
- Create or manage user accounts.
- Change roles or permissions.
- Access library administration unless explicitly included in their defined role, which it is not in this specification.
- Access department-only academic operations.
- Change fee structures or perform restricted financial actions merely because they are office members.

The User Management navigation item must not appear for office members.

### E. Library

The Library role can access library-related functionality only.

It must not display navigation items for:
- User Management.
- Fees Management.
- Office administration.
- Department accounts.
- Principal or Secretary administration.
- Other unrelated institutional modules.

Enforce the same restrictions on all relevant APIs.

### F. Faculty / Teacher Records

In this phase, faculty functionality is limited to maintaining teacher records.

Requirements:
- Maintain records of teachers working at the college.
- Do not create faculty login accounts or a faculty portal.
- Do not implement faculty dashboards, attendance, teaching workflows, or other faculty features in this phase.
- Teacher records must not automatically create login accounts.

Authorized Super Admin, Principal, Secretary, and office members may manage teacher records according to their institutional permissions.

Department accounts may manage teacher records belonging to their own department only, if the existing teacher-record functionality supports this operation.

Do not create duplicate teacher records or duplicate login accounts.

### G. Students

Implement or maintain student records as a shared institutional data source for integration with Fees Management and future ERP modules.

Requirements:
- No student login in this phase.
- No student portal.
- No student authentication accounts.
- Students are records, not login roles in the current implementation.
- Authorized Office Members, Principal, Secretary, and Super Admin can add and edit student records.
- Other roles must not receive student-record permissions unless explicitly specified in this document.
- Student records must be reusable by Fees Management and future modules.
- Do not duplicate student records across modules.

### H. Department Accounts

The Super Admin creates departments and assigns a department account to each department.

Requirements:
- Maintain one shared username/password account per department.
- Department accounts must be associated with a specific department in the database.
- Department users can access only their assigned department's records and marks.
- They must not access other departments' marks or restricted records.
- Department accounts may manage teacher records belonging to their own department.
- They must be able to use the department functions required for marks uploading and related academic operations when those features are implemented.
- Do not implement additional academic features beyond what is necessary for the User Management integration.
- Department accounts must not access User Management, Fees Management, Library administration, or institutional administration.

The shared account's department association must be enforced on the server. Never trust a department identifier supplied by the frontend without checking it against the authenticated account.

