## 10. Failed Login Lockout

Implement a temporary automatic lockout after six consecutive failed login attempts.

Requirements:
- Six consecutive failed attempts trigger a 30-minute lockout.
- During the lockout, valid credentials must not bypass the lockout.
- Record failed login attempts and lockout events appropriately.
- After the 30-minute period, allow login attempts again.
- Successful authentication resets the consecutive failure counter according to the established security policy.
- Prevent repeated requests from bypassing the lockout.
- Apply the same protection to username and email login.
- Return a generic and safe error message that does not expose sensitive account information.
- Do not require an administrator to unlock a normal 30-minute temporary lockout.

An administrator may still suspend or deactivate an account separately.

---

## 11. Single Active Session

Allow only one active session per account.

When a user logs in successfully on another device or browser:
- Invalidate the previous session.
- Create the new authenticated session.
- Ensure the previous session can no longer access protected APIs.
- Preserve CSRF protection and secure session handling.

Apply this rule to all account types.

Do not create exceptions for shared department accounts unless explicitly authorized by a later requirement.

